whoami -- Command

Print the login name of the effective user id
whoami

whoami prints, on one line, the login name that /etc/passwd gives for the
EFFECTIVE user id of the process.  It takes no arguments and reads no input.

This is not the question `who am i' answers.  That reads /etc/utmp and names
whoever logged this terminal in, which is still the login name after an su and
is no answer at all for a process with no terminal.  whoami reports the
identity the kernel would enforce, which is what matters when deciding whether
a command is about to be permitted.

    $ whoami
    guest
    $ su
    Password:
    # whoami
    root

Diagnostics
    whoami: no name for uid 25

No /etc/passwd entry has that user id.  The number is still the truthful
answer, so it is printed on the standard output as well, and the exit status
is 1.  Otherwise the status is 0.

